- RAM : 4 Go suffisent, le conteneur reste sous une limite stricte de 2 Go (mesuré)
- CPU : 2 cœurs suffisent, amd64 et arm64]]></description>
	<!--
		Both halves of Findling carry the same major and minor version, so that
		nobody ends up with a companion app and a backend that disagree about
		the protocol between them. The bump to 0.2.0 was the pull queue and the
		crawl; the bump to 0.3.0 was the fourth container route, GET /rates,
		which the admin page of this half reads for its estimate of the first
		index.

		The bump to 1.0.0 is the first store release, and it is a decision and
		not a consequence: D-11 names 1.0.0 as the version both halves are
		submitted under, and plan 05-17 is the only place in this phase that
		performs it. Nothing about the protocol changed with it.

		The three places that have to agree are unchanged: this element, the
		<version> of backend/appinfo/info.xml and the <image-tag> next to it.
		docker.yml compares all three against the git tag before it builds, so
		a bump on one side alone fails the release; backend/tests/
		test_lockstep_versions.py asks the same question on every ordinary run,
		which is the cheap half of the same guard.
	-->
	<version>1.0.2</version>
	<!--
		The SPDX spelling and not the short one. info.xsd accepts both, but it
		lists agpl, mit, mpl and apache under the heading Deprecated, and the
		spellings above them carry the schema comment "Requires Nextcloud
		minVersion >= 31". E-H1 of 2026-09-06 put min-version at 33, so that
		condition is met and the deprecated spelling has nothing left to buy.
		Raised on 07.09.2026 by plan 06.1-19, closing DI-06.1-05.

		This element moves in lockstep with the same element in the other half.
		Two store entries of one product that name different licences is the
		kind of contradiction the deferred item refused to risk by changing one
		side alone, so both were changed in one commit.
	-->
	<licence>AGPL-3.0-or-later</licence>
	<author>street1983nk</author>
	<namespace>Findling</namespace>
	<!--
		Three categories, and the first one is the one that was missing. The
		store has a "search" category, this app was in "files" only, so nobody
		browsing for a search app ever saw it. info.xsd declares category with
		maxOccurs="unbounded", so the number is free; 73 apps in the store carry
		three and 25 carry four.

		Why "search" first is not cosmetic. Measured against the store API on
		2026-09-04: the category page sorts by is_featured, then
		rating_num_recent, then last_release, and the "search" category has 15
		apps and NOT ONE featured app, while "files" has 142 apps of which 7 are
		featured and therefore unreachable. On the day of the submission that is
		place 1 of 16 here against place 8 of 143 there, for the same app and
		the same text.

		"tools" is the wide catch-all and adds little on its own, but it costs
		nothing and it is true of this app.
	-->
	<category>search</category>
	<category>ai</category>
	<category>files</category>
	<category>tools</category>
	<website>https://github.com/street1983nk/nextcloud-search</website>
	<bugs>https://github.com/street1983nk/nextcloud-search/issues</bugs>
	<repository type="git">https://github.com/street1983nk/nextcloud-search.git</repository>
	<!--
		The images of the store page. The store keeps addresses and not files,
		so they live in this public repository and are linked over https. The
		schema type is secure-url: https and at most 256 characters, which is
		what backend/tests/test_store_metadata.py checks, together with the one
		thing the schema cannot: that every address points at a file that
		actually exists under store/media. An address without an image behind
		it is an empty frame on the store page, and that is worse than no image.

		The branch and not a tag, on purpose. The images have to be reachable
		while the entry is reviewed and for as long as it stands, and a broken
		image should be fixable with a commit rather than with a new release.

		The order is the order the store shows them in, so the header image
		comes first. There is no separate field for it: a store page has
		screenshots and nothing else, and the first one is the one everybody
		sees.

		This half gets all three, because it is the half a person installs and
		searches with: the search image is the promise of the product, and the
		admin image is what whoever runs the instance wants to see first.

		store/media/README.md says what each image shows, how it was taken, and
		why it comes from the development instance rather than from CI.
	-->
	<screenshot>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/street1983nk/nextcloud-search/main/store/media/header.png</screenshot>
	<screenshot>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/street1983nk/nextcloud-search/main/store/media/screenshot-search.png</screenshot>
	<screenshot>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/street1983nk/nextcloud-search/main/store/media/screenshot-admin.png</screenshot>
	<!--
		The same donation link as the sister project of this author. The
		platform is named in the type attribute because the schema knows the
		value, which lets the store show the right destination instead of a
		bare address.
	-->
	<donation type="paypal">https://paypal.me/KhaledCherifDev</donation>
	<!--
		The server window of E-H1 of 2026-09-06, and it is the same one the other
		half declares: min-version moves from 32 to 33 because Nextcloud 32 leaves
		support in September 2026 while this app is submitted in December, and
		max-version stays at 35. Two entries in the store that named different
		servers would be two different promises about one app, which is why
		backend/tests/test_lockstep_versions.py compares this element with the one
		in backend/appinfo/info.xml and both with the matrix of
		.github/workflows/deploy-harp.yml.

		The PHP floor is untouched by that decision. It follows lib/versioncheck.php
		of the servers in the window, and 8.2 is the floor of 33 and 34; the entry
		for 35 in the matrix carries 8.3 for the same reason, which is a statement
		about the run and not about this app.
	-->
	<dependencies>
		<php min-version="8.2"/>
		<nextcloud min-version="33" max-version="35"/>
	</dependencies>
	<!--
		The two blocks below sit in the order the store schema prescribes, which
		is: dependencies, background jobs, the repair block, two factor
		providers, commands. Each of them is written on one line on purpose. The
		schema pattern for a PHP class name allows no surrounding whitespace, so
		an indented class name on its own line fails the validation.

		The install step is what makes the first index start without an admin
		doing anything, which is the whole zero config promise of this app. The
		first command is the way back out: it is the only way to rebuild the index
		short of uninstalling the app. The second one is the diagnosis of a single
		file without a browser, which is the way a support case takes: the admin
		route of this app demands the request token of a real browser session, so
		occ is the only access to that answer from a shell.

		The uninstall step is the other half of the same lifecycle, and it is
		here for a reason that is easy to get wrong: Nextcloud executes
		repair-steps/uninstall in AppManager::disableApp(), so this entry is
		reached by every disable and not only by a removal. That is why the step
		removes nothing unless an administrator stated the intent first, and why
		docs/uninstall.md carries the measurement instead of a claim.

		The third command belongs to that step and is the only place where an
		administrator states the intent to remove the data of this app. It is a
		command and not a button, because clearing the index bookkeeping is an
		operational procedure and not an everyday click, and because the admin
		page already carries one destructive confirmation with the cleanup of
		the exclusion rules.
	-->
	<repair-steps><install><step>OCA\Findling\Repair\AppInstallStep</step></install><uninstall><step>OCA\Findling\Repair\AppUninstallStep</step></uninstall></repair-steps>
	<commands><command>OCA\Findling\Command\IndexCommand</command><command>OCA\Findling\Command\DiagnoseCommand</command><command>OCA\Findling\Command\PurgeCommand</command></commands>
	<!--
		The settings block, written on one line for the same reason as the two
		above, and standing here because there is nowhere else to put it:
		IRegistrationContext has no registerSettings(), so the two classes
		cannot be registered in Application::register() the way the search
		provider and the event listeners are. Nextcloud reads these class names
		out of the installed appinfo/info.xml in AppManager::loadApp(), which
		makes this file the registration itself.

		The app store transform empties the block: pre-info.xslt has the
		templates for activity and settings swapped, so what the store
		validates is an empty <settings/>, and that passes because every child
		of the settings type carries minOccurs="0". It has no consequence,
		because the block is read at runtime from the installed file and never
		from the normalised copy the store keeps. The workflow step "State the
		settings finding explicitly" holds that finding and turns red the day
		it stops being true.

		The version above moved to 0.3.0 together with the two versions in
		backend/appinfo/info.xml, because plan 04-05 adds the container route
		GET /rates. All three are compared against the git tag in docker.yml, so
		a bump on one side alone fails the release before it builds.
	-->
	<settings><admin>OCA\Findling\Settings\Admin</admin><admin-section>OCA\Findling\Settings\Section</admin-section></settings>
</info>
